  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathrm{f}$ be derivable in $[0,1]$, then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "qceWa",
      "text": "there exists $c \\in(0,1)$ such that $\\int_0^c f(x) d x=(1-c) f(c)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "I33RU",
      "text": "there does not exist any point $d \\in(0,1)$ for which $\\int_0^d f(x) d x=(1-d) f(d)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "gison",
      "text": "$\\int_0^c f(x) d x$ does not exist, for any $c \\in(0,1)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "QsTkK",
      "text": "$\\int_0^c f(x) d x$ is independent of $c, c \\in(0,1)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $g(x)=x \\int_0^x f(t) d t-\\int_0^x f(t) d t$\nNow, $g(0)=0$\n$g(1)=0$\nBy Rolle's Theorem\n$g^{\\prime}(x)=0$\nfor some $x \\in(0,1)$\n$g^{\\prime}(x)=x f(x)+\\int_0^x f(t) d t-f(x)=0$",
    "image": null
  },
